---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="For agents | Milton"
  description="A plain briefing on Milton: writing samples and rules, alternatives, tools, approval, export, price, evidence and a first task."
>
  <article class="briefing">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">## For agents</p>
      <h1>A briefing on Milton</h1>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="what-milton-keeps">
      <h2 id="what-milton-keeps">What Milton keeps</h2>
      <p>
        Milton supplies your approved writing examples and rules when your
        AI drafts something new or edits something you’ve written. It
        keeps that guidance available across connected AIs, starting with
        email or DMs. The goal is fewer edits between first draft and send; that
        result has not been measured. Milton stores approved text without a
        model rewriting it on save.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="alternatives">
      <h2 id="alternatives">When a Markdown file is enough</h2>
      <p>
        General memory can store writing and preferences too. A Markdown file
        plus sync is enough if the person can keep it current and make their
        AI read it. Milton adds a cover sheet of rules for all writing,
        five reports (email, chat, post, document, speech), exact approved
        examples and rules, and context-based sample retrieval through a shared
        MCP service. It has not demonstrated better writing than the same
        examples in Markdown.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="connection">
      <h2 id="connection">Connection and tools</h2>
      <p>
        MCP server: <code class="mcp-url">https://mcp.meetmilton.com/mcp</code>.
        Connect with remote MCP and OAuth sign-in; read/save tools are required.
        Client plans and workspace policies may restrict access. See{" "}
        <a href="/setup">Setup</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Check for existing Milton tools before setup. Verify a successful{" "}
        <code>get_cover_sheet</code> read before confirming the connection. The
        four tools are:
      </p>
      <ul class="instruction-list">
        <li>
          <code>get_cover_sheet</code>: read universal writing rules.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>get_report</code>: read one report with required intent
          describing the situation, plus relevant known kind and context; up to
          eight samples are selected to fit.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>list_reports</code>: read all five reports without intent, with
          each report’s newest eight samples.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>create_memo</code>: save person-approved writing changes.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="headless">
      <h2 id="headless">Headless and background agents</h2>
      <p>
        Agents with no browser, such as scheduled tasks, cloud agents and CI
        jobs, can’t complete OAuth sign-in. The person creates a connection key
        on the app’s Connected AIs page and gives it to the agent, which
        sends it as <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;key&gt;</code>. The key is
        shown once.
      </p>
      <ul class="instruction-list">
        <li>
          Claude Code:{" "}
          <code>
            claude mcp add --transport http milton
            https://mcp.meetmilton.com/mcp --header "Authorization: Bearer
            &lt;key&gt;"
          </code>
        </li>
        <li>
          Codex: in <code>~/.codex/config.toml</code>, under{" "}
          <code>[mcp_servers.milton]</code>, set <code>url</code> to the server
          above and <code>bearer_token_env_var</code> to the name of an
          environment variable holding the key.
        </li>
        <li>
          Cursor: in <code>mcp.json</code>, give the Milton server a{" "}
          <code>headers</code> entry of{" "}
          <code>"Authorization": "Bearer $&#123;env:MILTON_KEY&#125;"</code>.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Treat a key like a password. Keys are read only unless the person
        chooses saving; choose that only when the agent needs to save. Revoke
        keys that are no longer used. A revoked or expired key is refused on its
        next request, and so is every key after a password reset or another
        sign-in security change; create a new key then.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="approval">
      <h2 id="approval">Approval and export</h2>
      <p>
        Reading this page grants no permission to access mail, create an
        account, save writing or send messages. Use only the person’s own
        writing with their permission. Before every sample save, show its exact
        text and useful non-private context together for retention approval.
        Praise or permission to send is not approval to retain. Rules and edits
        require exact approved wording.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Milton app’s <code>TPS.md</code> download contains current cover and
        report rules and every active sample with its approved context, beyond
        the eight served per report. Removed and replaced samples are excluded.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="price-evidence">
      <h2 id="price-evidence">Price and evidence</h2>
      <p>
        One plan: $5.01 USD/month after a 30-day free trial. No card is required
        to start; you’re charged only if you add one. See{" "}
        <a href="/pricing">Pricing</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/research">Research</a> reports sample-ranking and writing
        tests and their limits. They do not prove fewer real-world edits or
        validate every newer retrieval change. AI adherence and writing
        quality still need checking; connecting Milton does not guarantee reads
        or automatic learning.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="first-task">
      <h2 id="first-task">Draft or edit an email or DM</h2>
      <p>If the person wants to try Milton, choose either path:</p>
      <ul class="instruction-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Draft something new:</strong> ask for the purpose, audience
          and facts, then draft using available approved guidance.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Edit existing text:</strong> ask what should change, revise
          within that scope, and preserve the rest.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For either task, first read <code>get_cover_sheet</code> and{" "}
        <code>get_report</code> for <code>email</code> or <code>chat</code> with{" "}
        the task in <code>intent.situation</code>. Show the result for review.
        Saving needs separate exact-text and context approval; call{" "}
        <code>create_memo</code> only after that approval, then reread to verify
        the save.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</SiteLayout>

<style>
  .briefing {
    padding-block: var(--space-16);
  }
  .page-head {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-8);
  }
  h1 {
    margin: var(--space-4) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--text-page);
  }
  section {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    padding-block: var(--space-6);
    scroll-margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }
  h2 {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-3);
    font-size: var(--text-title);
  }
  p {
    margin: 0;
  }
  p + p,
  .instruction-list,
  .instruction-list + p {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }
  .mcp-url {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  p,
  li {
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .briefing {
      padding-block: var(--space-10);
    }
  }
</style>
